<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Native apps change their appearance when their window isn't the one the user is using: title bars fade,
selected text turns gray, and toolbar icons lose their color. It tells the user where their typing will
go. Installed web apps that draw their own title bar, using Window Controls Overlay
[[WINDOW-CONTROLS-OVERLAY]], can only do the same with script that listens for <code>focus</code> and
<code>blur</code> events.

<div class=example id=example-title-bar>
    <pre highlight=css>
    .title-bar { background-color: #1e3a8a; color: white; }

    @media (window-focus: inactive) {
      .title-bar { background-color: #e5e7eb; color: #6b7280; }
      ::selection { background-color: #d1d5db; }
      .toolbar img { filter: grayscale(1); }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

<div class=example id=example-game>
    A game pauses when its window loses focus, because it no longer receives key presses, and says so:

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (window-focus: inactive) {
      .paused-overlay { display: grid; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=feature>The '@media/window-focus' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: window-focus
Value: active | inactive
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/window-focus' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. In a [=document=] |d|, its
[=actual value=] is:

: <dfn value for=@media/window-focus>active</dfn>
:: |d|'s [=node navigable=]'s [=navigable/top-level traversable=] has [=top-level traversable/system focus=].
: <dfn value for=@media/window-focus>inactive</dfn>
:: Otherwise. This value evaluates to false in a [=boolean context=], so <code>(window-focus)</code>
    means active.

A top-level traversable whose document is hidden, such as a background tab, doesn't have system focus, so
its documents are ''window-focus/inactive''.

The value changes in the same task in which the user agent fires the <code>focus</code> or
<code>blur</code> event at the top-level traversable's active window because its system focus changed.
The user agent must not change it earlier.

<h3 id=frames>Frames</h3>

The feature uses [=frame-scoped exposure=] [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]]. Its policy-controlled feature is
"<dfn export>window-focus-state</dfn>", whose default allowlist is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>.

In a document |d| that isn't [=allowed to use=] "window-focus-state", the feature takes its
[=restricted value=]: ''window-focus/active'' if the [=has focus steps=] return true for |d|, and
''window-focus/inactive'' otherwise.

<div class=note>
    The restricted value is what <code>document.hasFocus()</code> returns in that document: whether the
    window is active and the user's focus is inside the frame. A cross-origin frame learns nothing it
    couldn't learn before. A chat widget or title bar in a cross-origin frame that should follow the
    window needs <code>allow="window-focus-state"</code>.
</div>

<h3 id=no-script>Documents without script</h3>

In a document for which <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#concept-n-noscript">scripting is disabled</a>, the feature is always ''window-focus/active''.

<h3 id=reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

In a [=reduced-exposure context=] in which scripting is enabled, the feature behaves as described above.

<div class=note>
    The feature reveals nothing that script in the same document can't already learn, so hiding it where
    script runs would protect nothing. The exception is documents without script, which the section above
    covers.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What the feature reveals</h3>

The feature reveals whether the user is using the window that shows the document, and, as the value changes,
when they switch to another window or app and back. That is a measure of attention: how long the user spent
with the page, and when they looked away.

It doesn't reveal which window or app the user switched to.

<h3 id=privacy-existing>What is already exposed</h3>

In a top-level document, script already learns exactly this:

* <code>document.hasFocus()</code> returns whether the window is active and focus is in the document.
* The window receives <code>focus</code> and <code>blur</code> events when the window gains and loses
    system focus.
* The <code>visibilitychange</code> event reports when the document is hidden, including, in many user
    agents, when another window covers it.

The feature takes the same value, and changes in the same task as those events, so script in a top-level
document learns nothing new, not even a more precise time.

In a cross-origin frame, script today learns only whether focus is inside the frame. It doesn't learn
whether the window is active while the user is using another part of the page. The restricted value keeps it
that way. See [[#privacy-third-party]].

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

The value is almost always ''window-focus/active'' when a page loads, because the user just opened it, and
it changes whenever the user switches windows. It adds no meaningful information to a stable fingerprint.

<h3 id=privacy-correlation>Linking visits by when the value changes</h3>

When the user moves from one window to another, one window becomes inactive and the other becomes active
at the same moment. A tracker present in both could link them by that moment.

* **Top-level documents.** The <code>blur</code> and <code>focus</code> events already give script in both
    documents that moment. The feature doesn't delay its change, because a delay would reveal nothing less
    to script, and a title bar that dims late looks broken.
* **Cross-origin frames.** Trackers that link visits across sites are usually embedded in those sites. With
    the restricted value, a frame's value changes only when focus enters or leaves that frame, which
    happens when the user clicks in it, not when they switch windows. A frame can't link the two windows.
* **Documents without script.** The value never changes. See [[#privacy-without-script]].

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Reading the feature without script</h3>

Without the rule in [[#no-script]], a style sheet could measure attention with no script at all: an email
could load one resource when its window became inactive and another when it became active again, and the
sender would learn how long the user read the message and when they looked away. HTML email is usually shown
with scripting disabled, so the rule makes the feature ''window-focus/active'' there, and it never changes.

Style sheets can already react to the user's attention in other ways, such as loading a resource on
<code>:hover</code>. The rule makes sure this feature doesn't add a new one.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

A cross-origin frame that isn't allowed to use "window-focus-state" sees whether focus is inside it, which
is what <code>document.hasFocus()</code> already tells it. The embedding page decides whether a frame needs
to follow the window, and can allow it for that frame alone.

<h3 id=privacy-inference>Sensitive inferences</h3>

Attention can be used to monitor people. Online exam software watches for the user leaving the test window,
and employers can watch how long workers spend in a web app. Script already makes this possible in
top-level documents, and the feature doesn't change that. It doesn't make monitoring possible in contexts
without script, or in embedded content.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* A frame that the embedding page allows to use "window-focus-state" learns what a top-level document does.
    Embedding pages should allow it only for frames that need it.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* The feature can't give a window focus or take it away.
* A page that draws its own title bar could use the feature to imitate a native window more convincingly.
    It can already do so with <code>focus</code> and <code>blur</code> events. The protections against
    imitation, such as the user agent's own title bar and the limits in Window Controls Overlay, are
    unchanged.
* A page can show content only while its window is inactive, when the user is less likely to notice it. It
    can already do so with script.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-pseudo-class>A '':window-inactive'' pseudo-class</h3>

WebKit has a nonstandard <code>:window-inactive</code> pseudo-class for selections and scrollbars. A
pseudo-class selects elements, and this is a fact about the environment, which is what media queries
describe. A media feature also works in <code>matchMedia()</code> and in HTML's <code>media</code>
attributes.

<h3 id=alt-top-level-everywhere>The window's state in every frame</h3>

Every frame could see whether the window is active. That would let third-party frames on two sites link the
user's windows by the moment focus moves between them, which they can't do today.

<h3 id=alt-display-state>Combining with minimized and maximized states</h3>

A single feature could describe focus along with whether the window is minimized, maximized, or full screen.
Those are independent: a maximized window can be inactive. Other proposals cover window states.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should a document that is visible but in an inactive window, such as a picture-in-picture
window the user isn't using, have a third value?

<p class=issue>Is ''window-focus/active'' the right value for documents without script, or should those
documents be allowed to follow the window when the user agent knows they aren't email?


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Whether the window showing the document is the active window, so pages can show that they are in the
    background.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. It exposes what <code>focus</code> and <code>blur</code> events already expose to top-level
    documents, and what <code>document.hasFocus()</code> already exposes to frames.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: It reveals attention, which script can already measure. See [[#privacy-inference]].
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: Frames and documents without script don't learn more than they already could.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: How long the user spends with the page and when they look away.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Only whether the window has system focus, which is already exposed.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: The moment focus moves between windows, which <code>focus</code> and <code>blur</code> events already
    expose to top-level documents. Frames and documents without script don't see it.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: Cross-origin frames get the restricted value unless allowed "window-focus-state". See [[#frames]].
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: The same as in normal browsing, because script can read the same information.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: When it is restored, it gets the current value, as <code>document.hasFocus()</code> does.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature that is safe for script, because script already has the information, becomes unsafe in
    contexts without script.
